  ("​| Val Int" 0.3258 0.3860 0.1774 0.1885 0.1885 0.0105 0.0088 0.3537 0.0047)
  ("​| Add (Exp v ) (Exp v )" 0.3258 0.4841 0.1927 0.2039 0.2039 0.0105 0.0046 0.3573 0.0047)
  ("eval :: Exp v → Env v → Int" 0.2381 0.4347 0.2115 0.2227 0.2227 0.0105 0.0046 0.4522 0.0272)
  ("eval (Var x ) γ = fetch x γ" 0.2381 0.4385 0.2275 0.2380 0.2380 0.0105 0.0049 0.5052 0.0272)
  ("eval (Val i )" 0.2381 0.3172 0.2429 0.2534 0.2534 0.0105 0.0046 0.3892 0.0272)
  ("γ=i" 0.3442 0.3812 0.2429 0.2534 0.2534 0.0105 nil 0.1073 0.0370)
  ("eval (Add p q) γ = eval p γ + eval q γ" 0.2381 0.5066 0.2583 0.2688 0.2688 0.0105 0.0046 0.3307 0.0272)
  ("where Env v is some notion of environment and fetch x projects the value for the" 0.1963 0.7988 0.2804 0.2910 0.2910 0.0105 0.0062 0.2385 0.0428)
  ("variable x ." 0.1963 0.2735 0.2958 0.3063 0.3063 0.0105 0.0056 0.3128 0.0572)
  ("We can eliminate the clutter of the explicitly threaded environment with a little" 0.2130 0.7988 0.3113 0.3219 0.3219 0.0105 0.0054 0.3069 0.0232)
  ("help from some very old friends, designed for this purpose:" 0.1963 0.6261 0.3267 0.3372 0.3372 0.0105 0.0056 0.2762 0.0307)
  ("eval :: Exp v → Env v → Int" 0.2381 0.4347 0.3484 0.3596 0.3596 0.0105 0.0046 0.4522 0.0272)
  ("eval (Var x ) = fetch x" 0.2381 0.4089 0.3645 0.3750 0.3750 0.0105 0.0047 0.5422 0.0272)
  ("eval (Val i )" 0.2381 0.3172 0.3799 0.3904 0.3904 0.0105 0.0046 0.3892 0.0272)
  ("=Ki" 0.3442 0.3846 0.3799 0.3922 0.3904 0.0105 nil 0.0001 0.0405)
  ("eval (Add p q) = K (+) ‘S‘ eval p ‘S‘ eval q" 0.2381 0.5386 0.3953 0.4076 0.4058 0.0105 0.0046 0.3770 0.0272)
  ("where" 0.1963 0.2391 0.4174 0.4279 0.4279 0.0105 nil 0.2393 0.0428)
  ("K :: a → env → a" 0.2548 0.3817 0.4288 0.4418 0.4400 0.0112 0.0046 0.5365 0.0130)
  ("Kx γ=x" 0.2548 0.3266 0.4449 0.4572 0.4554 0.0105 0.0067 0.3704 0.0254)
  ("S :: (env → a → b) → (env → a) → (env → b)" 0.4363 0.7746 0.4288 0.4418 0.4400 0.0112 0.0046 0.4891 0.0093)
  ("S ef es γ = (ef γ) (es γ)" 0.4363 0.6152 0.4449 0.4572 0.4554 0.0105 0.0060 0.2697 0.0093)
  ("Except for the noise of the K and S combinators^{2} , this definition of eval is in a" 0.2130 0.7988 0.4663 0.4805 0.4786 0.0105 0.0061 0.2794 0.0509)
  ("fairly standard applicative style, even though we are abstracting an environment." 0.1963 0.7918 0.4835 0.4940 0.4940 0.0105 0.0056 0.2397 0.0382)
  ("2 The Applicative class" 0.4072 0.5878 0.5249 0.5354 0.5354 0.0105 0.0064 0.2966 0.0096)
  ("We have seen three examples of this ‘pure function applied to funny arguments’" 0.1963 0.7988 0.5481 0.5587 0.5587 0.0105 0.0070 0.2505 0.0232)
  ("pattern in apparently quite diverse fields—let us now abstract out what they have" 0.1963 0.7988 0.5635 0.5740 0.5740 0.0105 0.0055 0.2680 0.0540)
  ("in common. In each example, there is a type constructor f that embeds the usual" 0.1963 0.7988 0.5789 0.5894 0.5894 0.0105 0.0060 0.2632 0.0140)
  ("notion of value, but supports its own peculiar way of giving meaning to the usual" 0.1963 0.7988 0.5943 0.6048 0.6048 0.0105 0.0060 0.2407 0.0465)
  ("applicative language—its idiom. We correspondingly introduce the Applicative class:" 0.1963 0.7988 0.6097 0.6202 0.6202 0.0105 0.0041 0.3360 0.0790)
  ("infixl 4 ~" 0.2381 0.3040 0.6321 0.6444 0.6426 0.0105 0.0046 0.3495 0.0361)
  ("class Applicative f where" 0.2381 0.4184 0.6508 0.6613 0.6613 0.0105 0.0047 0.3106 0.0354)
  ("pure :: a → f a" 0.2548 0.3598 0.6655 0.6767 0.6767 0.0105 0.0046 0.4728 0.0304)
  ("(~) :: f (a → b) → f a → f b" 0.2548 0.4693 0.6809 0.6921 0.6921 0.0105 0.0059 0.4900 0.0260)
  ("This class generalises S and K from threading an environment to threading an effect" 0.1963 0.7988 0.7037 0.7161 0.7142 0.0105 0.0043 0.3073 0.0326)
  ("in general." 0.1963 0.2726 0.7191 0.7296 0.7296 0.0105 0.0056 0.2328 0.0140)
  ("We shall require the following laws for applicative functors:" 0.2130 0.6477 0.7346 0.7451 0.7451 0.0105 0.0056 0.2985 0.0232)
  ("identity" 0.1963 0.2535 0.7571 0.7676 0.7676 0.0105 nil 0.2924 0.0573)
  ("pure id ~ u = u" 0.3910 0.5116 0.7571 0.7695 0.7676 0.0105 0.0047 0.3929 0.0304)
  ("composition" 0.1963 0.2882 0.7725 0.7830 0.7830 0.0105 nil 0.3422 0.0920)
  ("pure (·) ~ u ~ v ~ w = u ~ (v ~ w )" 0.3227 0.5892 0.7725 0.7849 0.7830 0.0123 0.0047 0.4495 0.0304)
  ("homomorphism" 0.1963 0.3126 0.7879 0.7984 0.7984 0.0105 nil 0.3161 0.1164)
  ("pure f ~ pure x = pure (f x )" 0.3604 0.5738 0.7879 0.8003 0.7984 0.0105 0.0047 0.3494 0.0304)
  ("interchange" 0.1963 0.2850 0.8033 0.8138 0.8138 0.0105 nil 0.2037 0.0887)
  ("u ~ pure x = pure (λf → f x ) ~ u" 0.3939 0.6477 0.8026 0.8156 0.8138 0.0105 0.0047 0.4259 0.0090)
  ("^{2} also known as the return and ap of the environment Monad" 0.1988 0.5768 0.8441 0.8542 0.8542 0.0084 0.0047 0.2605 0.0061))
  (4
  ("4" 0.1963 0.2046 0.1388 0.1494 0.1494 0.0105 nil nil 0.0084)
  ("Conor McBride and Ross Paterson" 0.3687 0.6263 0.1388 0.1494 0.1494 0.0105 0.0060 0.2641 0.0456)
  ("The idea is that pure embeds pure computations into the pure fragment of an" 0.2038 0.7988 0.1672 0.1778 0.1778 0.0105 0.0076 0.2295 0.0288)
  ("effectful world—the resulting computations may thus be shunted around freely, as" 0.1963 0.7988 0.1826 0.1931 0.1931 0.0105 0.0057 0.2897 0.0577)
  ("long as the order of the genuinely effectful computations is preserved." 0.1963 0.7059 0.1980 0.2085 0.2085 0.0105 0.0056 0.2393 0.0307)
  ("You can easily check that applicative functors are indeed functors, with the fol-" 0.2130 0.7988 0.2135 0.2240 0.2240 0.0105 0.0058 0.2322 0.0288)
  ("lowing action on functions:" 0.1963 0.3935 0.2289 0.2394 0.2394 0.0105 0.0056 0.2597 0.0469)
  ("(<$>) :: Applicative f ⇒ (a → b) → f a → f b" 0.2381 0.5652 0.2506 0.2617 0.2617 0.0105 0.0046 0.4869 0.0400)
  ("f <$> u = pure f ~ u" 0.2381 0.3851 0.2666 0.2790 0.2771 0.0105 0.0047 0.4209 0.0051)
  ("Moreover, any expression built from the Applicative combinators can be transformed" 0.1963 0.7988 0.2887 0.2992 0.2992 0.0105 0.0041 0.3269 0.0726)
  ("to a canonical form in which a single pure function is ‘applied’ to the effectful parts" 0.1963 0.7988 0.3041 0.3146 0.3146 0.0105 0.0048 0.2919 0.0149)
  ("in depth-first order:" 0.1963 0.3406 0.3195 0.3300 0.3300 0.0105 0.0056 0.2321 0.0140)
  ("pure f ~ u_{1} ~ . . . ~ u_{n}" 0.4179 0.5763 0.3388 0.3512 0.3493 0.0123 0.0037 0.4834 0.0304)
  ("This canonical form captures the essence of Applicative programming: computa-" 0.2130 0.7988 0.3583 0.3688 0.3688 0.0105 0.0058 0.2900 0.0326)
  ("tions have a fixed structure, given by the pure function, and a sequence of sub-" 0.1963 0.7988 0.3737 0.3842 0.3842 0.0105 0.0072 0.1841 0.0354)
  ("computations, given by the effectful arguments. We therefore find it convenient, at" 0.1963 0.7988 0.3890 0.3996 0.3996 0.0105 0.0053 0.2933 0.1033)
  ("least within this paper, to write this form using a special bracket notation," 0.1963 0.7446 0.4044 0.4149 0.4149 0.0105 0.0056 0.2692 0.0336)
  ("J f u_{1} . . . u_{n} K" 0.4478 0.5473 0.4279 0.4498 0.4384 0.0149 0.0028 0.4097 0.0067)
  ("indicating a shift into the idiom of an Applicative functor, where a pure function is" 0.1963 0.7988 0.4514 0.4619 0.4619 0.0105 0.0056 0.2765 0.0725)
  ("applied to a sequence of effectful arguments using the appropriate ~. Our intention" 0.1963 0.7988 0.4668 0.4791 0.4773 0.0105 0.0048 0.2845 0.0530)
  ("is to give an indication that effects are present, whilst retaining readability of code." 0.1963 0.7988 0.4821 0.4927 0.4927 0.0105 0.0049 0.2568 0.0112)
  ("Given Haskell extended with multi-parameter type classes, enthusiasts for over-" 0.2130 0.7988 0.4976 0.5082 0.5082 0.0105 0.0059 0.2741 0.0429)
